{:edb/db-before-t 884 :edb/db-after-t 885 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534197 50 #inst "2026-10-06T09:59:02.058Z" 13194139534197 true] [17592186128768 1100 "The area $A$ of a forest decreases with time due to deforestation. The rate of change of the forest area is proportional to the square of the area. What differential equation describes the rate of change of the area at time $t$?" 13194139534197 false] [17592186128768 1100 "The area $A$ of a forest decreases with time due to deforestation. The rate of change of the forest area is proportional to the square of the area. What differential equation describes the rate of change of the area at time $t?$" 13194139534197 true] [17592186128768 1145 "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The **area** $A$ of a forest **decreases** with time due to deforestation. The **rate** of change of the forest area is **proportional** to the **area squared**.  *\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *area* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm d t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm d t}$ is *proportional to* the *area squared.* In other words,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm d t} \\propto A^2.\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\propto$ means \"is proportional to.\"\n\n- Finally, we want to replace the proportionality symbol with an equality symbol. Let $k$ be a positive constant. Since the area *decreases* with time, our derivative should be *negative*. Therefore, we can rewrite our proportionality statement as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm d t} = -kA^2.\n$$" 13194139534197 true] [17592186128768 1145 "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n> *The **area** $A$ of a forest **decreases** with time due to deforestation. The **rate** of change of the forest area is **proportional** to the **area squared**.*\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *area* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity\n$\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm d t}$.\n- We're told that $\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm d t}$ is *proportional to* the *area squared.* In other words,\n$\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm d t} \\propto A^2$.\nRecall that $\\propto$ means \"is proportional to.\"\n- Finally, we want to replace the proportionality symbol with an equality symbol. Let $k$ be a positive constant. Since the area *decreases* with time, our derivative should be *negative*. Therefore, we can rewrite our proportionality statement as\n$\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm d t} = -kA^2$." 13194139534197 false] [17592186128769 1100 "The volume of water $V$ in a bucket decreases at a rate that's inversely proportional to the square of the volume of water. What differential equation describes the rate of change of the volume of water $V$?" 13194139534197 false] [17592186128769 1100 "The volume of water $V$ in a bucket decreases at a rate that's inversely proportional to the square of the volume of water. What differential equation describes the rate of change of the volume of water $V?$" 13194139534197 true] [17592186128769 1145 "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The  **volume of water** $V$  in a bucket **decreases** at a rate that's **inversely proportional** to the ** square of the volume of water** $V.$*\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *volume of water* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d V}{\\textrm d t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\dfrac{\\textrm d V}{\\textrm d t}$ is *inversely proportional to* the *square of the volume.* In other words,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d V}{\\textrm d t} \\propto \\dfrac{1}{V^2}.\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\propto$ means \"is proportional to.\"\n\n- Finally, we want to replace the proportionality symbol with an equality symbol. Let $k$ be a positive constant. Since the volume of water *decreases* with time, our derivative should be *negative*. Therefore, we can rewrite our proportionality statement as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d V}{\\textrm d t} = -\\dfrac{k}{V^2}.\n$$" 13194139534197 true] [17592186128769 1145 "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n> *The **volume of water** $V$ in a bucket **decreases** at a rate that's **inversely proportional** to the **square of the volume of water** $V$.*\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *volume of water* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity\n$\\dfrac{\\textrm d V}{\\textrm d t}$.\n- We're told that $\\dfrac{\\textrm d V}{\\textrm d t}$ is *inversely proportional to* the *square of the volume.* In other words,\n$\\dfrac{\\textrm d V}{\\textrm d t} \\propto \\dfrac{1}{V^2}$.\nRecall that $\\propto$ means \"is proportional to.\"\n- Finally, we want to replace the proportionality symbol with an equality symbol. Let $k$ be a positive constant. Since the volume of water *decreases* with time, our derivative should be *negative*. Therefore, we can rewrite our proportionality statement as\n$\\dfrac{\\textrm d V}{\\textrm d t} = -\\dfrac{k}{V^2}$." 13194139534197 false] [17592186128871 1145 "If the mass of the balloon is $M$ grams, then the rate of change of the mass is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}M}{\\textrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time in seconds.\n\nSince $20$ grams are lost every second, the rate of change of the balloon's mass is given by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}M}{\\textrm{d}t}=-20\n$$\n\nNotice that the right-hand side is *negative* because the balloon is *losing* mass." 13194139534197 false] [17592186128871 1145 "If the mass of the balloon is $M$ grams, then the rate of change of the mass is $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}M}{\\textrm{d}t},$ where $t$ is the time in seconds.\n\nSince $20$ grams are lost every second, the rate of change of the balloon's mass is given by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}M}{\\textrm{d}t}=-20.\n$$\n\nNotice that the right-hand side is *negative* because the balloon is *losing* mass." 13194139534197 true] [17592186128872 1100 "The volume of water $V(t)$ in a leaking tank decreases at a rate that is proportional to the time $t$ (in minutes) since the leak started. What differential equation describes the rate of change of the volume of water at time $t$?" 13194139534197 false] [17592186128872 1100 "The volume of water $V(t)$ in a leaking tank decreases at a rate that is proportional to the time $t$ (in minutes) since the leak started. What differential equation describes the rate of change of the volume of water at time $t?$" 13194139534197 true] [17592186128872 1145 "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The **volume** of water $V(t)$ in a leaking tank **decreases** at a **rate** that is **proportional** to the **time** $t$ (in minutes) since the leak started. *\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *volume* of water varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d V}{\\textrm d t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\dfrac{\\textrm d V}{\\textrm d t}$ is *proportional to* the *time* since the water begins to leak.  In other words,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d V}{\\textrm d t} \\propto t.\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\propto$ means \"is proportional to.\"\n\n- Finally, we want to replace the proportionality symbol with an equality symbol. Let $k$ be a positive constant. Since the volume of water *decreases* with time, our derivative should be *negative*. Therefore, we can rewrite our proportionality statement as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d V}{\\textrm d t} = -kt.\n$$" 13194139534197 true] [17592186128872 1145 "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n> *The **volume** of water $V(t)$ in a leaking tank **decreases** at a **rate** that is **proportional** to the **time** $t$ (in minutes) since the leak started.*\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *volume* of water varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity\n$\\dfrac{\\textrm d V}{\\textrm d t}$.\n- We're told that $\\dfrac{\\textrm d V}{\\textrm d t}$ is *proportional to* the *time* since the water begins to leak. In other words,\n$\\dfrac{\\textrm d V}{\\textrm d t} \\propto t$.\nRecall that $\\propto$ means \"is proportional to.\"\n- Finally, we want to replace the proportionality symbol with an equality symbol. Let $k$ be a positive constant. Since the volume of water *decreases* with time, our derivative should be *negative*. Therefore, we can rewrite our proportionality statement as\n$\\dfrac{\\textrm d V}{\\textrm d t} = -kt$." 13194139534197 false] [17592186129051 1020 #edb/ref 17592186281773 13194139534197 true] [17592186129051 1020 #edb/ref 17592186281777 13194139534197 true] [17592186129051 1020 #edb/ref 17592186281780 13194139534197 true] [17592186129051 1020 #edb/ref 17592186281815 13194139534197 true] [17592186129054 1020 #edb/ref 17592186281787 13194139534197 true] [17592186129054 1020 #edb/ref 17592186281794 13194139534197 true] [17592186129054 1020 #edb/ref 17592186281801 13194139534197 true] [17592186129054 1020 #edb/ref 17592186281808 13194139534197 true] [17592186129054 1020 #edb/ref 17592186281822 13194139534197 true] [17592186129189 1020 #edb/ref 17592186281717 13194139534197 true] [17592186129189 1020 #edb/ref 17592186281724 13194139534197 true] [17592186129189 1020 #edb/ref 17592186281731 13194139534197 true] [17592186129190 1020 #edb/ref 17592186281738 13194139534197 true] [17592186129190 1020 #edb/ref 17592186281745 13194139534197 true] [17592186129190 1020 #edb/ref 17592186281752 13194139534197 true] [17592186129190 1020 #edb/ref 17592186281759 13194139534197 true] [17592186129190 1020 #edb/ref 17592186281766 13194139534197 true] [17592186129577 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534197 true] [17592186129577 1100 "A skater is skating straight down a ramp. The displacement $D$ of the skater increases at a rate that's proportional to the square of the time $t$, in seconds.\n\nWhat differential equation describes the rate of change of displacement $D$ at time $t$, where $t$ is the time since he started skating?" 13194139534197 false] [17592186129577 1100 "A skater is skating straight down a ramp. The displacement $D$ of the skater increases at a rate that's proportional to the square of the time $t,$ in seconds.\nWhat differential equation describes the rate of change of displacement $D$ at time $t,$ where $t$ is the time since he started skating?" 13194139534197 true] [17592186129577 1145 "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*A skater is skating straight down a ramp. The **displacement** $D$ of the skater **increases** at a **rate** that's **proportional** to the **square of the time** $t,$ in seconds. *\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *displacement* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t}$ is *proportional to* the *square of the time.* In other words,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t}∝{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nRecall that $∝$ means \"is proportional to.\"\n\n- Finally, we want to replace the proportionality symbol with an equality symbol. Let $k$ be a positive constant. Since the displacement *increases* with time, our derivative should be *positive*. Therefore, we can rewrite our proportionality statement as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t}=k{t}^{2}.\n$$" 13194139534197 true] [17592186129749 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534197 true] [17592186129749 1100 "The value $V$ of a particular car decreases with time. The rate of change of the car's value is proportional to the square of the value. What differential equation describes the change of the value at time $t$?" 13194139534197 false] [17592186129749 1100 "The value $V$ of a particular car decreases with time. The rate of change of the car's value is proportional to the square of the value. What differential equation describes the change of the value at time $t?$" 13194139534197 true] [17592186129749 1145 "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The **value** $V$ of a particular car **decreases** with time. The **rate** of change of the car's value is **proportional** to the **value squared**.  *\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *value* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}$ is *proportional to* the *value squared.* In other words,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}∝{V}^{2}.\n$$\n\nRecall that $∝$ means \"is proportional to.\"\n\n- Finally, we want to replace the proportionality symbol with an equality symbol. Let $k$ be a positive constant. Since the value *decreases* with time, our derivative should be *negative*. Therefore, we can rewrite our proportionality statement as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=-k{V}^{2}.\n$$" 13194139534197 true] [17592186129750 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534197 true] [17592186129750 1145 "If the pines population is $P,$ then the rate of change of the population is $\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t},$ where $t$ is the time in years.\n\nSince $100$ new pines are added every year, the rate of change of the population of the pines is given by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=100.\n$$" 13194139534197 true] [17592186281717 1017 #edb/ref 17592186281718 13194139534197 true] [17592186281717 1089 "q-143029" 13194139534197 true] [17592186281717 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534197 true] [17592186281717 1100 "The population $P$ of mosquitos in a particular region increases with time. The rate of change of the population is proportional to the square of the population. What differential equation describes the rate of change of the population of mosquitos at time $t?$" 13194139534197 true] [17592186281717 1113 #uuid "f728f48a-b708-5919-9898-226ef8301050" 13194139534197 true] [17592186281717 1145 "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The **population** $P$ of mosquitos **increases** with time. The **rate** of change of the population of mosquitos is **proportional** to the **square of the population**.  *\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *population* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}$ is *proportional to* the *population squared.* In other words,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}∝{P}^{2}.\n$$\n\nRecall that $∝$ means \"is proportional to.\"\n\n- Finally, we want to replace the proportionality symbol with an equality symbol. Let $k$ be a positive constant. Since the population *increases* with time, our derivative should be *positive*. Therefore, we can rewrite our proportionality statement as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=k{P}^{2}.\n$$" 13194139534197 true] [17592186281718 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281719 13194139534197 true] [17592186281718 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281720 13194139534197 true] [17592186281718 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281721 13194139534197 true] [17592186281718 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281722 13194139534197 true] [17592186281718 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281723 13194139534197 true] [17592186281718 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534197 true] [17592186281718 1041 "selection" 13194139534197 true] [17592186281718 1104 #edb/ref 17592186281723 13194139534197 true] [17592186281718 1110 #uuid "da764f70-b364-59e6-9be0-62a1bbff0d89" 13194139534197 true] [17592186281719 1034 "\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=-k{P}^{2},\\,\\,k>0" 13194139534197 true] [17592186281719 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534197 true] [17592186281719 1111 #u