/ref 17592186045465 13194139534197 true] [17592186281801 1100 "The number of weekly sales of a particular book decreases with time. The number of units sold at any given moment decreases at a rate that's proportional to the number of units sold at that moment. What differential equation describes the rate of change of the number of units sold $V(t)$ at time $t,$ where $t$ is the time since the book first went on sale?" 13194139534197 true] [17592186281801 1113 #uuid "c7d02b61-80d9-5e06-9924-32034d7e7b9c" 13194139534197 true] [17592186281801 1145 "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The **number of units sold** at any given moment **decreases** at a **rate** that is **proportional** to **the number of units sold** at that moment.* \n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *number of units sold* of a particular book varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}$ is *proportional to* the *number of units sold.* In other words,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}∝V.\n$$\n\nRecall that $∝$ means \"is proportional to.\"\n\n- Finally, we want to replace the proportionality symbol with an equality symbol. Let $k$ be a positive constant. Since the number of units sold *decreases* with time, our derivative should be *negative*. Therefore, we can rewrite our proportionality statement as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=-kV.\n$$" 13194139534197 true] [17592186281802 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281803 13194139534197 true] [17592186281802 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281804 13194139534197 true] [17592186281802 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281805 13194139534197 true] [17592186281802 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281806 13194139534197 true] [17592186281802 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281807 13194139534197 true] [17592186281802 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534197 true] [17592186281802 1041 "selection" 13194139534197 true] [1759218